              "text": "The Mariana Trench in the western Pacific Ocean is the deepest place on Earth. At its lowest point, called the Challenger Deep, the seabed lies almost 11,000 metres below the surface. If Mount Everest were placed inside the trench, its peak would still be about two kilometres under water.\n\nConditions at the bottom are extreme. There is no sunlight at all, the water is close to freezing, and the pressure is more than a thousand times greater than at the surface. For many years, scientists believed that nothing could survive there.\n\nIn 1960, two men, Jacques Piccard and Don Walsh, decided to find out. They climbed into a special vessel called the Trieste, which had very thick steel walls and a tiny window. The journey down took almost five hours. When they finally reached the bottom, they stayed for only about twenty minutes, because a crack had appeared in one of the windows. They reported seeing a flat, fish-like creature on the seabed, although some scientists today doubt this.\n\nFor more than fifty years, nobody returned to the Challenger Deep. Then, in 2012, the film director James Cameron made the dive alone in a submarine he had helped to design. He spent about three hours exploring the bottom and filmed what he saw. Since then, several more expeditions have followed, and scientists have found tiny shrimp-like animals and other creatures living at great depths.\n\nUnfortunately, explorers have also found something less welcome: plastic. In 2019, an explorer saw a plastic bag lying on the seabed nearly 11 kilometres down. For many people, **this** was a shocking sign that human pollution has reached even the most remote places on our planet.",
